%I A007790 #37 Sep 06 2026 05:36:12
%S A007790 1,0,11,32,61,0,55,256,153,0,131,352,469,0,671,2048,817,0,379,1952,
%T A007790 605,0,551,2816,2825,0,1539,1760,2437,0,991,4096,1441,0,3355,4896,
%U A007790 3997,0,5159,15616,4921,0,1891,4192,9333,0,2255,22528,4753,0,8987,15008,8269
%N A007790 a(n) = (1/n)*Sum_{x in (Z/nZ)^4} e^(2*Pi*i*det(circ(x))/n), the normalized Gauss sum of the 4 X 4 circulant determinant.
%C A007790 From _Zoltan Reti_, Aug 29 2026: (Start)
%C A007790 For a vector x = (x_0, x_1, x_2, x_3) let circ(x) be the 4 X 4 circulant matrix whose (i,j) entry is x_{(j-i) mod 4}, so that each row is the previous row shifted one place to the right. Its determinant is a form of degree 4 in 4 variables, namely det(circ(x)) = (x_0+x_1+x_2+x_3)*(x_0-x_1+x_2-x_3)*((x_0-x_2)^2+(x_1-x_3)^2). Put that determinant in the exponent of an additive character and average over all residue vectors modulo n. This is a multiple Gauss sum: its exponent is a form in four variables, in place of the single square of the classical one-variable Gauss sum.
%C A007790 a(n) values are nonnegative integers for all n. The a(n) function is multiplicative, but not strictly multiplicative.
%C A007790 At an odd prime p the value depends only on p mod 4:
%C A007790   a(p) = 3*p^2-3*p+1 if p == 1 (mod 4);
%C A007790   a(p) = p^2+p-1 if p == 3 (mod 4);
%C A007790 The ramified prime is p = 2,
%C A007790   a(2) = 0,
%C A007790   a(2^k) = 2^ceiling(5*k/2) for 2 <= k <= 3,
%C A007790   a(2^4) = 2*2^10, and
%C A007790   a(2^k) = (k-4)*2^(2*k+2) for k >= 5.
%C A007790 At k = 4 the break is invisible in the cofactor, since 2*2^10 = 2^11 is still a pure power; that is why the geometric head appears to run one step longer than it does.
%C A007790 a(n) = 0 if and only if 2 exactly divides n.
%C A007790 Context: the same construction for every degree d gives a family of sequences, of which this is d = 4; see A007789 (d = 3), A007791 (d = 5), A007792 (d = 6). The reference below determines every value of a(n) here, and more generally determines g_d(n) for every n whenever (d <= 256 except d = 128) or (d is not divisible by the fourth power of a prime).
%C A007790 (End)
%D A007790 B. C. Berndt, R. J. Evans and K. S. Williams, Gauss and Jacobi Sums, Wiley, 1998, p. 549.
%H A007790 Zoltan Reti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21969894">The Multiple Gauss Sums Behind A007789-A007792</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%H A007790 Zoltan Reti, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.21970261">Multiple Gauss Sums Generated by Circulant Matrices</a>, Zenodo, 2026 (Volume I of the two-volume work, third edition); the master formula (Theorem 4.2), the prime-power evaluation (Theorem 5.12), the exact wild law at p || d (Theorem 6.3), the law for d = p^2 (Theorem 6.30), and the tower factorization, lattice-index census and completeness scan of Sections 6.11-6.13.
%H A007790 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Circulant_matrix">Circulant matrix</a>.
%H A007790 Wikipedia, <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gauss_sum">Gaussian sum</a>.
%F A007790 From _Zoltan Reti_, Aug 29 2026: (Start)
%F A007790 Multiplicative: a(n1*n2) = a(n1)*a(n2) for gcd(n1,n2) = 1.
%F A007790 If p = 2:
%F A007790   a(2) = 0, a(2^2) = 2^5, a(2^3) = 2^8, a(2^4) = 2^11; a(2^k) = (k-4)*2^(2*k+2) for k >= 5.
%F A007790 If p = 1 (mod 4):
%F A007790   a(p^k) = (((k+1)*(k+2)/2)*p^2 - k*(k+2)*p + (k*(k+1)/2)) * p^(2*k-2).
%F A007790 If p = 3 (mod 4):
%F A007790   a(p^k) = (((k+1)/2)*p^2 + p - ((k+1)/2)) * p^(2*k-2) if k is odd;
%F A007790   a(p^k) = (((k+2)/2)*p^2 - k/2) * p^(2*k-2) if k is even.
%F A007790 (End)
%e A007790 a(5) = 61: here 5 == 1 (mod 4), so u^4-1 splits completely over GF(5) and a(5) = 5^3 - 4^4/4 = 125 - 64 = 61.
%e A007790 a(3) = 11: here 3 == 3 (mod 4), the factor degrees are 1, 1, 2, and a(3) = 3^3 - 2*2*8/2 = 27 - 16 = 11.
%e A007790 a(15) = 671: 15 = 3*5, so a(15) = a(3)*a(5) = 11*61 = 671 by multiplicativity.
%e A007790 a(16) = 2048
%t A007790 dv[dd_, p_] := Sort[Flatten[Table[With[{f = MultiplicativeOrder[p, e]}, Table[f, {EulerPhi[e]/f}]], {e, Divisors[dd]}]]];
%t A007790 gt[dd_, p_, k_] := Module[{fs = dv[dd, p], pr, T, z}, pr = PadRight[CoefficientList[Normal[Series[Product[(1 - p^-f)/(1 - (z/p)^f), {f, fs}], {z, 0, k}]], z], k + 1]; T[a_] := 1 - Sum[pr[[m + 1]], {m, 0, a - 1}]; p^((dd - 1)*k)*(p*T[k] - T[k - 1])/(p - 1)];
%t A007790 g[p_, k_] := If[p == 2, Which[k == 1, 0, k <= 3, 2^Ceiling[5 k/2], k == 4, 2^11, True, (k - 4) 2^(2 k + 2)], gt[4, p, k]];
%t A007790 a[n_] := If[n == 1, 1, Times @@ (g @@@ FactorInteger[n])];
%t A007790 Table[a[n], {n, 1, 53}] (* _Zoltan Reti_, Aug 29 2026 *)
%o A007790 (PARI) a(n) = {my(h=vector(n)); forvec(x=vector(4,i,[0,n-1]), h[(matdet(matrix(4,4,i,j,x[((j-i)%4)+1]))%n)+1]++); sumdiv(n, e, moebius(n/e)*h[(e%n)+1])/n} \\ direct from the definition, feasible for small n; _Zoltan Reti_, Aug 29 2026
%Y A007790 Cf. A007789 (d=3), A007791 (d=5), A007792 (d=6). The analogous sequences for d = 7, 8, 9, 10 are being submitted separately; cross-references will be completed later.
